course: everyday
lesson: 462
title: Sleepovers and inside jokes
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Lily sleeps over at Maddie's for the first time. At 2am they are still whispering under the duvet, crying with laughter at the word «spatula». «LIGHTS OFF, YOU TWO,» Maddie's dad bellows up the stairs. They finally drop off at three and spend Sunday as grumpy zombies. Maddie's mum is from Newcastle: «Howay, pet, time for bed,» she says — and Lily doesn't get it until Maddie explains that «howay» is Geordie for «come on», and «pet» means «love». By Monday Lily is saying «howay» at home and her mum is baffled.
explain: Sam and Jake have an inside joke: whenever anyone says «potato», they both shout «PO-TAH-TO» like a posh butler. Nobody else gets it, which is the point. Then the teacher says «potato» in science and they explode laughing and get moved apart. Inside jokes and secret handshakes — bump, spin, clap, finger guns — tie friends together. When three other kids copy the handshake on Sports Day, Sam is half annoyed and half proud. Chloe and Aisha keep a shared diary of jokes and drawings, until Chloe's brother reads it out at dinner; Mum takes his phone for a day, and the diary moves to Aisha's house. Lily keeps a jar of notes from Maddie — silly drawings, «u r the best», a lolly stick — and reads them on sad days.
explain: At midnight at a sleepover, Chloe gets homesick and wants to go home. «Don't be such a baby,» says one girl. Chloe phones her mum anyway. Next time she stays till eleven and goes home for bed, and nobody mentions it. At another sleepover Mia's friends dare her to ring the last number in her mum's phone and say something rude. Mia says no. They call her «boring» for five minutes and then forget. Kieran picks a horror film rated 15; Connor has nightmares for a week and won't admit it, until his mum phones Kieran's mum.
explain: Kieran wets the bed at a sleepover. His best mate Tom spots it, says nothing, swaps sleeping bags and spills a drink «by accident» on the wet one. Nobody else ever finds out. Kieran never forgets. That's a friend of the very best kind. Meanwhile Jake's mum bans sleepovers for a month because he was rude to his teacher. «Mum's being proper mardy,» Jake moans — Midlands and Northern English for grumpy. Jake says sorry to his teacher, and next month the sleepover is back on.
fact: At a sleepover the house rules belong to the host's parents, including bedtime.
fact: Feeling homesick at a sleepover is normal, and going home early is fine; a good friend says «that's alright, see you tomorrow».
fact: Inside jokes and secret handshakes help friends feel close, but save them for times they won't get you into trouble.
fact: Films at a sleepover should suit the age of everyone there; age ratings like 15 exist for a reason.
fact: Saying no to a dare that could hurt someone or get you in trouble is being a good friend to yourself.
fact: Private things between friends, like a shared diary, need a safe place; if someone breaks that, tell a grown-up.
fact: A real friend protects you when you are embarrassed, by being kind, not by hurting anyone else.
fact: When a parent cancels plans after a real problem, fixing the problem works better than blaming the parent.
fact: Keeping small reminders of good times, like notes or photos, can help on sad days.
fact: Every family speaks a bit differently, and asking what a word means is part of being friends.
fact: In Newcastle «howay» means «come on» and «pet» is a friendly word like «love»; in the Midlands and the North «mardy» means grumpy or sulky.
exercise: Why did Maddie's dad shout up the stairs? | It was 2am and they were still awake | They had broken the bed | They were playing football inside on the landing | They wanted pizza
exercise: Whose rules count at a sleepover? | The host's parents' rules | The guest's own rules | Whoever is oldest | Nobody's, it's a sleepover
exercise: «Howay, pet, time for bed.» What does «howay» mean? | Come on | Go away | Hello | Goodnight
exercise: Why do Sam and Jake shout «PO-TAH-TO»? | It's their inside joke | They are learning French | The teacher told them to | They are allergic to potatoes
exercise: What happened when the teacher said «potato» in science? | They burst out laughing and got moved apart | They got a house point each for listening well | The teacher joined in | Nobody noticed
exercise: Chloe got homesick at midnight. What did a good friend's answer sound like? | «That's alright, see you tomorrow» | «Don't be a baby» | «You're not allowed to go» | «We'll tell everyone»
exercise: Is it normal to feel homesick at a sleepover? | Yes, lots of kids do | No, only babies feel that | Only if the house is cold | Only the first time ever
exercise: Why did Connor have nightmares for a week? | They watched a horror film rated 15 | He ate a lot of cheese just before bed | Kieran's dog howled all night | He fell off the bunk bed
exercise: What did Mia do about the rude phone call dare? | Said no | Did it but whispered | Made her friend do it | Rang the police instead
exercise: What happened after Mia said no? | They called her boring for five minutes, then forgot | They never spoke to her again | She got sent home | They all got grounded
exercise: How did Tom protect Kieran after the wet bed? | Swapped bags and spilled a drink on the wet one | Told everyone it was the dog that had done it | Laughed loudly so that Kieran would laugh too | Woke up the parents
exercise: «Mum's being proper mardy.» What does «mardy» mean? | Grumpy and sulky | Very kind | Very tired | Very funny
exercise: Why did Jake's mum ban sleepovers? | He was rude to his teacher | He wet the bed | She doesn't like his friends | It was too expensive
exercise: How did Jake get his sleepover back? | He apologised to his teacher | He shouted until she gave in | He hid her car keys | He went anyway
exercise: Chloe's brother read her private diary aloud at dinner. What's a good next step? | Tell a grown-up and keep it somewhere safe | Read his diary out at the next dinner to get even | Never write anything again | Laugh along and forget it
exercise: Why does Lily keep a jar of notes from Maddie? | To cheer herself up on sad days | To sell them later | To show the teacher | Because Maddie asked her to hide them
test: «she's crying for her mum at midnight, cringe». What's the kind response? | Homesick is normal, let her go home if she wants | Make her stay till morning so she learns to cope | Film her for the group chat | Tell her she's ruined it
test: Kieran: «it's only a 15, we're basically 15 anyway». What's the problem? | The film isn't meant for their age and can scare kids badly | Nothing, ratings are just numbers | 15 means 15 minutes long | Films don't affect anyone
test: «go on, ring the last number and say summat rude, don't be a wet wipe». Best move? | Say no and laugh it off | Do it but hang up fast | Make someone else do it | Ring your teacher instead
test: Someone copies your secret handshake at Sports Day. Should you be furious? | No, it means you made something cool | Yes, report them to the head | Yes, invent a new handshake every day | No, but stop being friends with your mate
test: Imagine Tom had laughed and told everyone about the wet bed. What would most likely happen? | Kieran would be humiliated and the friendship hurt | Kieran would find it funny and laugh along with them | Nobody would care at all | Kieran would thank him
test: «why's lily saying howay at home, she's not even from newcastle». What happened? | She picked up a Geordie word from Maddie's mum | She made it up herself | It's a word from a game she plays online with mates | She's moving to Newcastle
test: In a cartoon, kids stay up all night every night with no problems. What's real? | You end up exhausted and grumpy the next day | Kids don't need sleep | Staying up makes you smarter | Only adults get tired
test: Jake: «mum's being well unfair, i'm not saying sorry». What would most likely get the sleepover back? | Saying sorry to his teacher | Sulking harder until Mum gives in | Asking his dad behind her back | Sleeping at a friend's anyway
test: The teacher says «potato» and you and your mate start wheezing with laughter. Best idea? | Save the joke for break time | Shout PO-TAH-TO louder | Throw a potato | Explain the joke to the class
test: Which is a sign of a really good friend at a sleepover? | They quietly help you when you're embarrassed | They pick the scariest film so you look brave | They dare you the hardest | They stay up longest
test: «can't believe ur mum's mardy again». Which word means the same as «mardy»? | Grumpy | Generous | Sleepy | Hungry
exam: Identify the party whose rules govern the conduct of guests at a sleepover. | The parents of the host | The eldest guest present | Each guest according to his own household | No party whatsoever, on that occasion
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises homesickness experienced by a child at a sleepover? | It is a normal experience and an early return home is acceptable | It indicates immaturity requiring ridicule | It is experienced exclusively by infants | It obliges the child to remain until morning
exam: Given that guests at a sleepover propose a telephone call of a rude nature, ascertain the most appropriate response. | To decline to participate | To participate whilst concealing one's identity | To compel another guest to make the call | To make the call to a teacher instead
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the protection of a friend from embarrassment. | A true friend may protect him kindly without harming any other person | A true friend publicises the matter for amusement | Such protection is a form of dishonest conduct to be avoided | Embarrassment is best remedied by ridicule
